<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mathura Lion Capital</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> Lion Capital is a sandstone column capital carved with two lions, found at Mathura in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. Its inscriptions in Kharoshthi record donations to the Buddhist community by the chief queen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajuvula">Rajuvula</a> and name other members of the ruling family. It is a key source for the Saka rulers of Mathura.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mathura-lion-capital.jpg" alt="Mathura Lion Capital" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anthony Huan · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mathura_lion_capital,_Asia,_G33_South_Asia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
